# The 8 MB of octal PSRAM an S3R8 carries in package, layered on top of
# sdkconfig.defaults. Leaving it out is the S3FN8 configuration, which
# is what a Cardputer would give us.
#
# STATUS: the DevKitC-1 N16R8 here has bad PSRAM. Espressif's own test
# settles it - components/esp_psram/test_apps/psram, built with their CI
# configuration sdkconfig.ci.esp32s3_octal_release, hangs on this board
# at the same instruction ours does. Nothing above the hardware is left
# to blame.
#
# The shape of the fault: the PSRAM answers on its configuration
# registers - vendor id 0x0d (AP), 64 Mbit, good-die pass - and the MSPI
# timing tuning completes and picks a point. The first real data access
# then stalls the bus. The boot hangs inside esp_psram_extram_test(),
# which cpu_start runs right after starting the second core, and the RTC
# watchdog resets the board. The log line that reports a failed test
# never prints, because the test does not return to print it.
#
# What was ruled out on the way there:
#   - our code: IDF's hello_world, same options, comparable image size,
#     hangs at the same instruction
#   - the speed: 40 MHz behaves exactly like 80 MHz
#   - the second core: single-core mode moves the hang, not removes it
#   - the memory test alone: with CONFIG_SPIRAM_MEMTEST=n the boot
#     reaches heap_init and stalls where the pool is added to the
#     allocator, which is simply the next real access
#   - the configuration: Espressif's compatibility table puts quad flash
#     at 80 MHz SDR and octal PSRAM at 80 MHz in the same permitted
#     group, and the v0.2 errata list carries nothing about PSRAM, SPI
#     RAM or MSPI
#
# esphome/esphome#15907 reports the same failure on the same N16R8
# designation, down to the "Embedded PSRAM 8MB (AP_3v3)" line esptool
# prints, and is unresolved there too.
#
# So this file is correct and waits for a board whose PSRAM works.
# Everything the port needs runs without it.
CONFIG_SPIRAM=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_OCT=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_SPEED_80M=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_USE_MALLOC=y

# Left at the IDF default deliberately. Zero lets the interpreter's
# small allocations reach PSRAM, which is the only way it would benefit
# from having any, but it also lets allocations that must stay internal
# out into external memory.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_MALLOC_ALWAYSINTERNAL=16384
